---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import { business, plan, money, monthlies, offerSchema } from '../data/business';

const title = 'Pricing — Wardith';
const description =
  'Wardith pricing in plain numbers: a £250 audit, an £800 one-off Foundation setup, and ongoing monthly plans at £150 (Maintain), £400 (Grow) and £700 (Lead). No lock-ins.';

const audit = plan('audit');
const foundation = plan('foundation');
const maintain = plan('maintain');

const serviceSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: business.serviceName,
  serviceType: business.serviceType,
  provider: { '@id': new URL('/#organization', Astro.site).href },
  areaServed: business.areaServed,
  description:
    'One service in three stages: a £250 audit of how AI assistants currently answer questions about your business, an £800 one-off Foundation setup that makes your business readable and credible to those systems, and ongoing monthly plans that keep you visible.',
  offers: offerSchema(),
};

// Just the offers, lifted out of the object above — the whole Service block
// would bury the prices in surrounding boilerplate.
const offersOnly = { '@type': 'Service', name: business.serviceName, offers: offerSchema() };
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={[serviceSchema]}>
  <section class="hero hero--rules">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>What does Wardith cost?</h1>
      <p class="lede" data-reveal>
        One service, three stages, plain numbers: diagnose the problem, fix the foundations, then
        keep checking. You start with a {money(audit.price)} audit, and every stage after that is
        only worth buying if the one before says so.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="price-block" data-reveal>
        <div class="price-grid">
          <div class="price-head">
            <h2>Audit</h2>
            <span class="price">{money(audit.price)} <small>one-off</small></span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p>
              A written report on where your business currently stands with the major AI assistants:
              ChatGPT, Google's AI results, Copilot and Perplexity. We ask them the questions your
              customers ask, record what comes back, and diagnose why. You'll have the report within
              two working days of us confirming scope and payment.
            </p>
            <p>You get:</p>
            <ul class="ticks">
              <li>What each assistant currently says when asked about businesses like yours.</li>
              <li>What they know and believe about your business, and whether it's accurate.</li>
              <li>
                Where your own details disagree with each other across the web: your Google
                Business Profile, Bing Places, Companies House, your professional register, the
                main directories and your social pages, each recorded exactly as it has you.
              </li>
              <li>The specific things blocking you, in plain English.</li>
              <li>Our honest recommendation — including "you're fine, don't spend more money."</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="price-block" data-reveal>
        <div class="price-grid">
          <div class="price-head">
            <h2>Foundation</h2>
            <span class="price">{money(foundation.price)} <small>one-off</small></span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p>
              The setup that fixes the underlying issues your audit found, done on the website you
              already have. It is a fixed piece of work, not an open-ended one: crawler access for
              the AI systems, structured machine-readable facts about your business, your facts made
              consistent everywhere they appear online, and <strong>two permanent pages</strong>
              answering questions your customers ask. Your audit decides which two. If the biggest
              thing in your way sits outside those four, the audit says that instead.
            </p>
            <p>
              We'll come back to you within two working days of your payment clearing, with a plan
              and a date. How long the work itself takes depends mostly on you — how quickly we get
              access to your website and the information we need. We'll tell you what we're waiting
              on, and we won't leave you guessing.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="price-block" data-reveal>
        <div class="price-grid">
          <div class="price-head">
            <h2>Ongoing</h2>
            <span class="price">from {money(maintain.price)} <small>per month</small></span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p>
              The assistants change constantly, so staying visible is ongoing work. The three levels
              do different jobs: hold your position, close the gaps, or close them faster across a
              wider set of questions. You can move between them at any
              time. What separates them is how much ground we cover each month and how many
              permanent pages we write for you, because a page is the part that keeps working after
              we've written it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <ul class="levels">
          <li class="level">
            <span class="level-name">Maintain</span>
            <p>
              <strong>Best if you mainly want to protect what the Foundation built and know where
              you stand.</strong> Every month we ask the assistants ten questions your customers
              ask, five times each because their answers vary, and send you a short
              written record: where you appeared, what changed since last month, and which questions
              you're still missing from. Your business facts stay current, and we fix them when they
              drift.
            </p>
            <span class="level-price">{money(monthlies[0].price)} /month</span>
          </li>
          <li class="level">
            <span class="level-name">Grow</span>
            <p>
              <strong>Best if the audit found clear gaps and you want them actively closed.</strong>
              Everything in Maintain, across fifteen questions instead of ten. Each month we take
              one of the questions you're missing from and write the page that answers it properly,
              on your own site — yours to keep, working whether or not you stay with us. That's how
              you go from showing up sometimes to showing up for the things people actually ask.
            </p>
            <span class="level-price">{money(monthlies[1].price)} /month</span>
          </li>
          <li class="level">
            <span class="level-name">Lead</span>
            <p>
              <strong>Best in a competitive market, where you want gaps closed faster and want to
              know who is being named ahead of you.</strong> Twenty-five questions a month and two
              new answer pages, so coverage builds at roughly twice the rate of Grow across a wider
              set of questions. Every quarter you also get a written review of the competitors the
              assistants are naming ahead of you, and our honest read on why.
            </p>
            <span class="level-price">{money(monthlies[2].price)} /month</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="price-block" data-reveal>
        <div class="price-grid">
          <div class="price-head">
            <h2>The same list, in machine form</h2>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p>
              Every price above is also published in a form built for machines, produced from the
              same numbers as the list, so the two can't disagree. That is exactly the job Wardith
              does for clients, and you don't need to read a line of it to buy anything.
            </p>
            {/* Folded away 2026-08-09. The block itself was never the argument
                — the sentence above is — and on a page whose job is to let
                somebody decide what to spend, forty lines of JSON sitting open
                mid-column is weight rather than evidence. It stays one click
                from the claim it supports, which is what evidence is for. The
                homepage keeps its record panel open: there the code *is* the
                subject of the section. Native <details>, so it opens with no
                script and the whole block is still in the page source. */}
            <details class="disclose">
              <summary>Show the machine-readable version</summary>
              <Code
                caption="The prices, as an AI assistant reads them"
                label="Wardith's prices as JSON-LD offers"
                data={offersOnly}
                note="Abridged from this page's structured data — the offers, without the surrounding business details. The prices above are what you pay. Wardith isn't VAT registered, so there's no VAT to add."
              />
            </details>
            <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
              Monthly plans roll month to month with no minimum term — tell us before your next
              payment date and there won't be one. The month you've paid for runs to the end; we
              don't refund part-months.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--invert">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div data-reveal>
        <h2>Why does the audit cost {money(audit.price)}?</h2>
      </div>
      <div data-reveal>
        <p>
          Because it's done by hand, for your business specifically. We put your customers' actual
          questions to each assistant repeatedly, because their answers vary run to run and asking
          once tells you nothing. We record what comes back, word for word, including every
          competitor named ahead of you. Then someone reads all of it and works out why.
        </p>
        <p>
          The report stands on its own. You could act on it yourself, or take it to someone else,
          and it would still be worth having. We'd rather charge properly for real work than price
          it as a sales call with a document attached.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
